# elisa-ui widget constants, shared by both widget layers. Each closed set is a
# CONST ENUM rather than a module of constants: a value reads `UiConst::Axis.Row`, the
# member separator is `.` because a const enum is a type and not a module, and a
# typo is a compile error ("enum has no variant") instead of an undefined name or
# -- where a bare constant is used as a match arm -- a binding that silently
# matches everything. A const enum IS its backing, so every ordinal and every
# boundary is unchanged.
#
# The sets live in `UiConst` rather than at file scope, which is what lets the
# names drop the `Ui` they used to carry: the module supplies it, and both
# widget layers reach them the same way (`UiConst::Axis.Row`). `WidgetEvent`
# and `WidgetKind` keep their `Widget`, because `UiConst::Event` beside
# `UiCore::Event` would read as the same thing scoped two ways, and they are
# not the same thing at all -- one is a click report, the other is input.
#
# The separators differ: `::` reaches into the module, `.` names a variant of
# the type it found there.

module UiConst:
    public:
        # A container distributes its children ALONG one axis and stretches them
        # ACROSS the other -- wxBoxSizer's orientation, spelled as a value rather
        # than a class.
        const enum Axis of i32:
            Column = 0
            Row = 1

        # Placement on the axis perpendicular to a parent's layout direction.
        # Stretch preserves the framework's original behavior; the other modes
        # keep the child's measured size and place it within the available band.
        const enum CrossAlignment of i32:
            Stretch = 0
            Start = 1
            Center = 2
            End = 3

        # Distribution of children along their parent's layout direction once
        # minima, growth, maxima, margins, and explicit spacing are satisfied.
        const enum MainAlignment of i32:
            Start = 0
            Center = 1
            End = 2
            SpaceBetween = 3

        # What a widget layer reports back to the application through app_widget_event.
        #
        # That callback keeps taking a plain `i32`: it is the APP CONTRACT, forwarded to
        # C as elisa_ui_on_widget_event, and every application and test implements it.
        # So the ordinal converts at the call, exactly as the wire kinds do at the C
        # boundary -- the named set lives here, the boundary stays an integer.
        const enum WidgetEvent of i32:
            Click = 0
            Change = 1
            Submit = 2

        # What a flat-layer widget IS. The sealed hierarchy in ui_hierarchy.elisa carries
        # this in its type instead, which is the better answer; the flat layer needs a
        # tag, and a named one keeps `new_widget(parent, UiConst::WidgetKind.Button)` from being
        # a bare 1. It is also what a native controls backend will switch on to decide
        # which OS control to create, so it wants to be a closed set the compiler checks.
        const enum WidgetKind of i32:
            Panel = 0
            Button = 1
            Label = 2
            RadioButton = 3
            CheckBox = 4
            Slider = 5
            ProgressBar = 6
            TextField = 7
            Scroll = 8
